592186045417] [17592186101683 #edb/ref 1061 16190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101683 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "880e64ac-57e9-5d82-9ecc-43590af3ef76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101683 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101684 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101684 #edb/ref 1061 16191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7473014-2f99-59a1-b6cb-a5a8ad7926aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101685 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101685 #edb/ref 1061 16193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101685 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cad7c8a-e8e1-5ac0-bdff-f4886c348fb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101686 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101686 #edb/ref 1061 16194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101686 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a8c2469-c4d7-571d-a243-2cf1fe09ed32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101686 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101699 #edb/ref 1061 17078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101699 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "acbf3f49-a158-5e75-b433-69f7dd172d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101699 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101708 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101708 #edb/ref 1061 18564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101708 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7a9c822-fdf0-5e24-ac81-c8d6d9320645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101708 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101709 #edb/ref 1061 18565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18c41421-e014-54c1-bddd-7391ac9c146f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101710 #edb/ref 1061 18615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101710 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8d2cea34-9028-5c66-b57d-e1c8dde4c2ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101710 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101711 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101711 #edb/ref 1061 18616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101711 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5010d59b-20f1-5abe-9dbf-e1b6304f1342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101711 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101961 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0.$ For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3,$ we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 23\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A \\cdot I_3 = A.$ You can check that $I_3 \\cdot A = A$ using the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general: \n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1,$ namely that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\n for any real number $a.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186101961 #edb/ref 1065 2995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101961 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5383efb1-6e51-562a-bf79-9ab8cba87ed7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101961 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101962 #edb/ref 1064 "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A.$\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2,$ the result is the second column of $A.$\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186101962 #edb/ref 1065 2996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101962 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2c46b879-fe8a-5f8e-8579-71b1bc92ac07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101962 #edb/ref 1067 "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column of the Identity Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101965 #edb/ref 1064 "We've been using the fact that $AI_n = I_n A = A$ for any $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ But how do we know for sure?\n\nLet's prove the case of $n=2.$ That is, let's prove that $AI_2=I_2A = A,$ where $A$ is any $2\\times2$ matrix.\n\nAny general $2 \\times 2$ matrix can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n\nThen, multiplying by $I_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11}\\cdot 1+{a}_{12}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{11}\\cdot 0+{a}_{12}\\cdot 1 \\\\ {a}_{21}\\cdot 1+{a}_{22}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{21}\\cdot 0+{a}_{22}\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above proof shows that the property $A I_2 = I_2 A = A$ holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $A.$\n\nIn general, to prove that the property $AI_n = I_n A = A$ holds for all $n \\times n$ matrices $A,$ we can use the same method." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186101965 #edb/ref 1065 3132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101965 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c2408f2-4c4d-5bc6-bd0c-e649f5e542f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101965 #edb/ref 1067 "Proving Properties Related to the Identity Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101973 #edb/ref 1064 "Given the parametric equations of a curve, we can find its Cartesian equation by eliminating the parameter.\n\nFor instance, consider the parametric curve given by\n\n$$\nx=2t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2 \\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$\n\nTo find the Cartesian equation of the curve, we start with the $x$ equation and solve for $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad t=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute this into our expression for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3.$\n\nHowever, our parametric curve was subject to the constraint $-2\\leq t \\leq 2.$ Since $x=2t,$ the corresponding constraint for $x$ is $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3, \\quad -4 \\leq x \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nThe graph of this curve is shown below:\n\n![](images/46/4697114d3ce40272a49dff794c4375301ae346e81b823049f587f8780172c413.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101973 #edb/ref 1065 3354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101973 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3f03b65a-1f29-5eb4-839d-45eb293997a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101973 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101974 #edb/ref 1064 "When parametric equations contain trigonometric equations, we can often eliminate the parameter using a trigonometric identity.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos t, \\,\\,\\, y = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sin t, \\quad 0 \\leq t\\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nFor the sine and cosine functions, we have the following Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\, & \\Rightarrow \\,\\operatorname{cos}t=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}t\\, & \\Rightarrow \\,\\operatorname{sin}t=3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+(3y)^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $4x^2 + 9y^2 = 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101974 #edb/ref 1065 3360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101974 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ac28915-be9b-5357-8577-3e4d1ab9a25d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101974 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Curves Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105708 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105712 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105712 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105712 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921861057