/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106760 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106760 #edb/ref 1061 27299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106760 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d66903ed-322e-5a89-82cf-d007695d5d4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106760 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106761 #edb/ref 1061 27300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106761 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec55bd0b-4d12-5185-b2f3-c2f5b6e10834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106761 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106762 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106762 #edb/ref 1061 27301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106762 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c81f0294-20be-52d5-b339-bc0866cf7bdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106762 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106763 #edb/ref 1061 27302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8be7abf-649f-5403-aa2f-6829e8676c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106793 #edb/ref 1061 28351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0cfde5a4-e03f-5b7b-b7a3-084b4e188e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106831 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106831 #edb/ref 1061 38089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106831 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ee2b1f28-9218-55e7-85d6-9d0eb6abb586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106831 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106832 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106832 #edb/ref 1061 38090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106832 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e5fcbe4-37c4-5421-b3a8-e040c80da52d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106832 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107065 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ below. How can we determine the intervals where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative?\n\nThe first step is to identify the points where the derivative is zero. Here, the critical points are $x=2$ (a local maximum) and $x=4$ (a local minimum), and $f'(x) = 0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/de/de01a8dec747f86d6b276431ef02675d3827028508be9aa9ca3699ca4e6bcd6c.png)\n\nThe next step is to identify where the function is increasing and decreasing. Below, the intervals where $f(x)$ is increasing are shown in blue, and the intervals where $f(x)$ is decreasing are shown in green.\n\n![](images/10/10edc10cefa3cc48713795e0e2f40b05ba6174e2da7aa5698a02fc35444b1c45.png)\n\nSince we know where the function is increasing or decreasing, we can determine where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative.\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are positive, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)>0.\n$$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are negative, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)<0.\n$$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be  summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,2) & 2 & (2,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{'} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, \n\n- $f'(x)>0$ on $(-\\infty,2) \\cup (4,+\\infty),$ while\n\n- $f'(x)<0$ on $(2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107065 #edb/ref 1065 2661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107065 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a75bc2f2-f4fa-50c8-9eaa-230212f35132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107065 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107067 #edb/ref 1064 "To determine whether a function $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing, we can calculate the derivative and then solve the appropriate inequality.\n\n- To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we solve the inequality $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107067 #edb/ref 1065 2665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107067 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9bdb8755-6e15-5d0b-b4da-b1a720b6dc4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107067 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing or Decreasing Using Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107074 #edb/ref 1064 "The **scalar triple product** of three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is defined as $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFor example, suppose we have three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 1,1,-2 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle 0,3,1 \\rangle.$ Let's compute their scalar triple product, $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFirst, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =7i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨7,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The scalar triple product takes its name from the fact that it multiplies three vectors together and always results in a scalar.\n\nLater, we will learn some geometric intuition behind the scalar triple product. But for now, we will focus on computing the scalar triple product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107074 #edb/ref 1065 2952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107074 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28eff4c1-2c88-5799-b15d-0ab9f27be09b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107074 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107075 #edb/ref 1064 "It turns out that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nThat is to say, if $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's focus on computing the scalar triple product using the determinant above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107075 #edb/ref 1065 2953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107075 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "67915540-ba40-5a4a-a7b0-6d81497d64c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107075 #edb/ref 1067 "The Definition of the Scalar Triple Product Via the Determinant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107076 #edb/ref 1064 "The scalar triple product has two important properties.\n\nFirst, scalars can be factored out of the product. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{a}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot ({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{c}) = {\\color{red}2}\\left[ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\right].\n$$\n\nSecond, if we swap exactly two vectors in the scalar triple product, the result changes sign. For example, swapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\cdot ( {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = - {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\cdot ( {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nNote that if we swap twice, then the result remains the same, because the negatives cancel out. For example, swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}$ and then swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-b\\cdot (a\\times c) \\\\  & =-(-b\\cdot (c\\times a)) \\\\  & =b\\cdot (c\\times a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese properties arise naturally from the fact that the scalar triple product can be computed as a determinant.\n\n1\\. If we multiply a row of the matrix by a number, then the determinant is multiplied by that number:\n  ![](images/82/827b78de142e085b00c9c7eccb43ba32b30861ef45d91b53b1f35c86a67b78f4.svg)\n\n2\\. If we swap two rows, then the determinant changes sign:\n  ![](images/ac/acadadda23e53448493a31441e2d3e53e89b6a320d6b4040c7567a2489d3cff3.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107076 #edb/ref 1065 2954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107076 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "41cd1550-dd91-5029-a397-fa1417d1e160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107076 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of the Scalar Triple Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107077 #edb/ref 1064 "We have been using the fact for three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ the scalar triple product can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut why is this true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\cdot (|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k) \\\\  & ={a}_{1}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|-{a}_{2}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|+{a}_{3}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107077 #edb/ref 1065 2955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107077 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "99f752d5-13c8-5b3e-aeee-383770b0db33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107077 #edb/ref 1067 "A Proof of the Formula for the Scalar Triple Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107080 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/6a/6a69daf7712312876b0ec97c540bbc472e46a7938beb8a59e694a04ad0b1d8c6.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/a8/a8bfe3f6b27247ee9b27db8346fd94fb9068c85dec6f4a71517a821dee48dbf3.svg)\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n![](images/0b/0ba4a232f29dd6fce2a8f6d7cda39b4f5b192c20fc91fe4403dfe648231382c5.svg)\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186107080 #edb/ref 1065 3153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107080 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0c91df0b-40c7-5a65-9fda-5d2d6438dfa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107080 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107081 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear equations, we have the following possibilities for the number of solutions:\n\n- The system has exactly *one solution* if and only if the respective coefficient matrix is non-singular (meaning that the determinant is not zero). Such systems are called **consistent independent**.\n\n- The system has *infinitely many solutions* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s. These systems are called **consistent dependent**.\n\n- The system has *no solution* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s on the left-hand side and a non-zero element on the right-hand side. These are called **inconsistent** systems.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\n**Note:** A system of linear equations of the form \n![](images/cb/cb397dd01794f28ff11c01079e287d63175e439c8bbaa85cd02dc61689780fa8.svg)\nwhere we have only $0$'s on the right-hand side, is called **homogeneous**. A homogeneous system always has at least one solution, namely, the solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107081 #edb/ref 1065 3154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107081 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b885f832-9e76-5008-a764-7375b1c25e35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107081 #edb/ref 1067 "The Number of Solutions of a 2x2 System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n![](images/63/63e2c65a23a0680412a14084d543b6cc19c0529fd80863b09c99987d309eaa4f.svg) \nand the vector ![](images/b7/b7811b74d8d3075357925864b5be27f57b292dadca9c04d222954baa1e66ba2a.svg) Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector ![](images/13/13a28e543647ede7a88b30fb652bd7fba6c9adb8ee3142161a102de947ef2d2a.svg) is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1065 3557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1014a8e8-38ce-568b-86d7-22f32ef8f697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107097 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n![](images/e8/e8c64d8be247afd3e8ab02abc69e33635ef0234dbcbbc60f42379021a39a58a2.svg)\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186107097 #edb/ref 1065 3901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107097 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "20c281f5-a455-581a-a7f7-fec6cfaa114e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107097 #edb/ref 1067 "Singular Systems with No Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1064 "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues can be interpreted geometrically.\n\nFirst, recall that if the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is the standard matrix of some linear transformation ![](images/80/80d488fc9d8168cecdf882f1aa178325bb36bd387dd9f86f2b0cd98a5c54f4dc.svg) then the product ![](images/dc/dcc52971e9a7e86ebbe8bf972c562fce13d389f9261b69cba05dcabb1426b9dc.svg) gives the image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/a1/a1af41bdb2f2fe410c6f8e02d1a2a0048f0aa470ceef4508753090444ca039be.svg) The image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) can appear quite different from the original vector ![](images/92/9221d4dc55c1df8da019956ea23f9d4f9ff3f5b04921ec2158804a31a50e636b.svg)\n\nHowever, for an eigenvector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ee/eed93a562368727b490c2b63d14f46580dcd0439ac90df23a23f75a31de9e971.svg)\n\nIn other words, when the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is applied to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the image of ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is a scaled version of the original vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg), and the scale factor is ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider once again the following matrix:\n\n![](images/43/43e76f5a5e889ec18a6893be8cdbf4837d3dedfab149a0981ce6619a62718170.svg)\n\nWe've already seen that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has an eigenvector ![](images/fe/fef256ade4f52003da67fac5af68ff4b0d4e7f04ef55e72bb0279bdc8f1e8c3c.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/b5/b5cdbd2f286044241daca2565a1ab7f70196ecc2cf6503965d9a820faad9b06e.svg) So we have\n\n![](images/2d/2da94bfdde0e3650ded2622748ead5a4c09cabf610f9d181d48ae158d9bd0986.svg)\n\nPlotting the vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and its image ![](images/ab/abe8027351c1645f4c582440b8b536f48f3ec35d008570f3432f322fa468e861.svg) in the plane, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9a/9aca41e0597b0b1698e8ec24da9966fd9b7e5fee3951081d8abdb0cc0caaac7b.png)\n\nIn general, when we apply a matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the resulting vector ![](images/be/befecb9d885ee3bbbb7313401ca3671428e4aced68a4bfdb4b2fc9a005ea2b43.svg) always lies on the same line as ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/8c/8c453358dacec49655b560be52a99066e3cab1d34cfbdbdbaa7bd74d868fef82.svg) then the matrix stretches the vector. For ![](images/ea/ea6d742051f82ff2f258e08ed09c3b3dc6d4c7f9bbbe40fea718aec8183dc44f.svg) the matrix compresses the vector. In addition, a negative eigenvalue ![](images/c9/c91cda6703c5767aaafeca807b07e466f160cb0ade84472ade5fa85d71a5713e.svg) means that ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is flipped in the opposite direction as well as scaled." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1065 3963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cdf6ca75-e37b-552a-a106-ee8ac2f36a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1067 "The Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107103 #edb/ref 1064 "A **random variable** is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nFor example, suppose we conduct an experiment where we flip a coin twice. The sample space for this experiment, which we'll denote as $\\Omega$ (the Greek capital letter \"Omega\") is given below:\n\n$$\n\\Omega = \\left\\{HH, HT, TH, TT\\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we define the random variable $X$ as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $X =$ \"*the number of heads we get when a coin is flipped twice*.\" \n\nLet's work out the possible values of $X.$\n\n- If we get two heads (i.e. the outcome of the experiment is an element of $\\{HH\\}$), then $X = 2.$\n\n- If we get one head (i.e. the outcome of the experiment is an element of $\\{HT,TH\\}$), then $X = 1.$\n\n- If we get no heads (i.e. the outcome of the experiment is an element of $\\{TT\\}$), then $X = 0.$\n\nSo, the possible values of $X$ are given by the following set:\n\n$$\nS = \\{0,1,2\\}\n$$\n\nInformally, if we flip a coin twice and count the number of heads, we can get either no heads, one head, or two heads. No other values are possible.\n\nThe set $S$ is called the **range** or **support** of $X,$ and it includes *every possible value* of the random variable $X.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107103 #edb/ref 1065 4440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107103 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0e1db88b-1f70-5049-bcce-35015bbe268c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107103 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107115 #edb/ref 1064 "The **probability distribution**, or more precisely, **probability mass function** of a discrete random variable $X$ on a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ that maps each element $x \\in S$ to its probability of occurring:\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe above can be read as \"the probability that the random variable $X$ takes on the value $x,$ is equal to $f(x).$\"\n\nFor example, if $X$ is the result of rolling a $6$-sided die, then we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & x=1,2,3,4,5,6 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function tells us that\n\n- the probability of the die landing on any integer between $1$ and $6$ inclusive is $\\dfrac{1}{6},$ and\n\n- the probability of the die landing on any other number is zero.\n\nWe can also represent this probability mass function using a table, as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107115 #edb/ref 1065 4609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107115 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "66daf81e-f474-50f8-b670-e55e24eeb1db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107115 #edb/ref 1067 "The Probability Mass Function for a Discrete Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107132 #edb/ref 1064 "A **discrete random variable** is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nNote that an interval of real numbers cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nFor example, the result of rolling a $6$-sided dice is a discrete random variable because its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\nOn the other hand, the temperature outside is *not* a discrete random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. The temperature could be anywhere between, say, $50^\\circ \\, \\textrm F$ and $90^\\circ \\, \\textrm F,$ and the interval of real numbers $[50, 90]$ cannot be listed in a sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107132 #edb/ref 1065 6286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107132 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "41fdd07b-af71-5dd7-8541-e3c161774315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107132 #edb/ref 1067 "Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107133 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function for a random variable $X$ on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to the conditions above.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$ states that any possible outcome for $X$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ must add up to $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107133 #edb/ref 1065 6287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107133 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e714d0c9-9e63-52c5-a365-16afc370e98e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107133 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditions for a Function To Be a Valid Probability Mass Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114877 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b6054f5d-d816-5aba-ad9f-7006e0de6f71" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
